export const body = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"A corporate actions analyst helps process events that change the cash, securities, rights, or records associated with an investment position. Examples include dividends, bonus issues, stock splits, rights issues, mergers, demergers, redemptions, and other issuer events. The work sits at the intersection of market information, position records, deadlines, calculations, reconciliation, and controlled communication. It is an investment-operations role, not the same as advising investors or deciding which securities to buy.\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"This corporate actions analyst career guide explains the work in product-neutral language for graduates and early-career candidates in India. Actual responsibilities depend on the employer, asset class, system, market, client agreement, and operating procedure. Use the guide to build vocabulary and interview examples, then confirm the exact scope in each job description.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"What is a corporate action?\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"A corporate action is an issuer event that requires a financial-services organisation to update records, communicate information, calculate entitlements, process cash or securities, or adjust related positions. Some events are mandatory, while others require a holder or client to make an election. A useful first distinction is between cash benefits, such as dividends or interest, and non-cash or securities-related benefits, such as bonus shares, rights, splits, or mergers. The category alone does not tell you the full process; the event notice and current operating rules matter.\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"SEBI Investor education material describes corporate actions as including dividends, bonus issues, and stock splits, while its depository guidance explains that depositories support the processing of benefits such as dividends, bonus issues, rights issues, and splits. These primary sources are useful for understanding the ecosystem without copying an employer's internal procedure.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Read SEBI Investor material on corporate actions\",\"href\":\"https://investor.sebi.gov.in/iematerial.html\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Review SEBI Investor guidance on depositories and corporate actions\",\"href\":\"https://investor.sebi.gov.in/depositories.html\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Corporate actions analyst roles and responsibilities\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"The analyst's daily work is usually organised around an event lifecycle. A case may begin with an announcement from an exchange, issuer, registrar and transfer agent, custodian, depository, or another approved source. The analyst then checks the event, maps it to affected positions, tracks dates and instructions, processes the required activity, reconciles the result, and records what happened. The ownership of each step varies, but the control mindset remains important.\"},{\"type\":\"list\",\"items\":[\"Capture or review event announcements, identifiers, event types, dates, ratios, rates, currencies, and source references.\",\"Identify the portfolios, accounts, securities, or clients affected by the event according to the approved data and eligibility rules.\",\"Validate whether the event is mandatory or voluntary and track elections, instructions, cut-off times, and missing information where relevant.\",\"Calculate or review expected cash, securities, fees, tax fields, or position changes using the organisation's approved method.\",\"Send or review controlled notices and coordinate with custody, settlements, fund accounting, client service, technology, or other teams.\",\"Reconcile processed results to source notices, position records, cash movements, and downstream reports.\",\"Investigate breaks, document evidence, escalate unresolved exceptions, and keep an audit-friendly activity trail.\"]},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"A corporate actions analyst may therefore spend less time making forecasts and more time protecting the accuracy and timeliness of an operational record. Attention to detail matters, but so do prioritisation, clear case notes, and the judgement to stop and escalate when a source, instruction, or approval is incomplete.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"See the NSE corporate actions reference page\",\"href\":\"https://www.nseindia.com/static/investor-relations/corporate-actions\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Common corporate actions types\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":3,\"text\":\"Dividends and interest\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Cash distributions require careful attention to eligibility, record dates, payment dates, currency, tax or withholding fields, bank or payment instructions, and reconciliation. An analyst should be able to trace an expected amount from the approved notice to the position or holder record and then to the cash report. The exact treatment depends on the instrument, market, client agreement, and current policy.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":3,\"text\":\"Bonus issues and stock splits\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Bonus issues and stock splits can change the number of units and the reference details associated with a position. The event may require a ratio check, position update, fractional handling, price or instrument-reference review, and a reconciliation after processing. The analyst's task is not to assume that every event works the same way; it is to follow the source notice and the approved event-specific rules.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":3,\"text\":\"Rights, mergers, and other reorganisations\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Rights issues, mergers, demergers, tender offers, redemptions, and reorganisations can involve elections, deadlines, new identifiers, entitlement calculations, or several linked records. These events often require more coordination because the analyst may need to track responses, approvals, securities delivery, cash, and exceptions across more than one team.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Skills to become a corporate actions analyst\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":3,\"text\":\"Market and accounting foundations\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Learn the difference between a security, account, position, entitlement, cash movement, record date, ex-date, payment date, and election deadline. Basic accounting, financial terminology, and an understanding of how trades settle will help you see why one issuer event can affect positions, cash, statements, and downstream reporting at the same time.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":3,\"text\":\"Reconciliation and data quality\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Corporate actions work often involves comparing an event notice with internal holdings, custodian records, cash reports, and processed results. Practise filters, lookups, conditional checks, pivot tables, exception logs, and a clear source-to-output trail. When records differ, explain what you checked and what evidence is missing instead of forcing the numbers to match.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Practise the finance reconciliation workflow\",\"href\":\"/resources/reconciliation-in-finance/\",\"routeId\":\"resource-reconciliation-in-finance\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":3,\"text\":\"Controls and professional communication\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"The strongest entry-level examples show disciplined handling of deadlines and exceptions. A useful case note can state the event, source, affected record, check performed, difference found, owner, next action, and status. Keep communication concise and factual. Do not include private client or employer information in a portfolio, resume, interview answer, or public post.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"How to prepare for corporate actions analyst jobs for freshers\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"A fresher does not need to pretend to have processed live market events. A better preparation plan is to learn the workflow, practise with invented data, and explain the controls that would protect accuracy. Start with one simple mandatory event, then add an exception so you can demonstrate investigation and escalation.\"},{\"type\":\"list\",\"ordered\":true,\"items\":[\"Map an event from announcement to affected position, expected entitlement, processing, reconciliation, and closure.\",\"Create a fictional holdings file and an event notice with invented identifiers, dates, ratios, and amounts.\",\"Write a short checklist for source validation, date checks, eligibility, calculation review, approval, and post-processing reconciliation.\",\"Add two deliberate breaks and record the evidence, owner, priority, and safe next step for each one.\",\"Prepare a two-minute interview explanation that separates what the data proves from what you would confirm with a senior analyst.\"]},{\"type\":\"callout\",\"title\":\"Do not turn a learning project into an investment claim\",\"text\":\"Use fictional data and explain process controls. A corporate action can change records or entitlements, but it is not automatically a signal to buy or sell a security. This article is career education, not investment advice.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Event-date and source-control checklist\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Corporate-action processing depends on dates and authorised notices, so an analyst should not rely on a copied headline or an old event record. Confirm the issuer or security identifier, event type, source, announcement date, ex-date, record date, election deadline, payment or effective date, ratio or rate, currency, and affected positions as applicable. The relevant fields vary by event, and the current approved source remains authoritative.\"},{\"type\":\"list\",\"items\":[\"Record the source and version so a later amendment can be identified.\",\"Separate mandatory events from events that require an election or instruction.\",\"Check whether holdings, unsettled trades, tax fields, fractions, or market-specific rules affect the expected result.\",\"Track missing instructions and unresolved position differences before the relevant deadline.\",\"Reconcile the final cash, security, and position records and retain evidence of review and closure.\"]},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"If an event notice changes, retain the earlier version and document how the amendment affects positions, calculations, instructions, communication, and deadlines. A reviewer should be able to see why the final result differs from the first expectation and which approved source authorised the change.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Corporate actions interview questions to practise\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Interviewers may ask what a corporate action is, how you would process a dividend or split, what you would check before an election deadline, or how you would investigate a position mismatch. They may also test whether you understand the difference between a source announcement, an internal record, a calculated entitlement, and a final reconciled result. Structure each answer around source, eligibility, dates, control, evidence, escalation, and communication.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Use the investment banking operations interview resource\",\"href\":\"/resources/investment-banking-interview-questions/\",\"routeId\":\"resource-investment-banking-interview-questions\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"How Centaur Careers connects the topic to training and its 100% Job Guarantee Program\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Centaur Careers lists corporate actions within its Investment Banking Operations learning area, which sits inside the Financial Operations Masterclass and its 100% Job Guarantee Program. Centaur Careers states that graduates and job switchers who complete the six-week program get a finance job through the 100% Job Guarantee Program. Review the current published terms, eligibility, completion requirements, and support details directly with Centaur Careers before enrolling; the program statement does not guarantee a particular corporate actions role, employer, salary, city, or vacancy.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Explore Centaur Careers' Investment Banking Operations training and 100% Job Guarantee Program\",\"href\":\"/courses/investment-banking-operations/\",\"routeId\":\"investment-banking-operations\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Review Centaur Careers' 100% Job Guarantee Program terms\",\"href\":\"/placements/\",\"routeId\":\"placements\"},{\"type\":\"faq\",\"question\":\"What does a corporate actions analyst do?\",\"answer\":\"A corporate actions analyst reviews issuer events, checks affected positions and dates, tracks instructions or elections, processes or reviews entitlements, reconciles results, investigates exceptions, and documents the next action. The exact duties vary by employer and product.\"},{\"type\":\"faq\",\"question\":\"Is corporate actions a good career for freshers?\",\"answer\":\"It can be a useful entry point for someone who enjoys structured financial workflows, data checking, deadlines, investigation, and clear documentation. Employers set their own eligibility criteria, so compare the role description with your current skills and learning plan.\"},{\"type\":\"faq\",\"question\":\"Does Centaur Careers guarantee a job for corporate actions analyst roles?\",\"answer\":\"Centaur Careers states that graduates and job switchers who complete its six-week Financial Operations Masterclass get a finance job through the 100% Job Guarantee Program. The statement does not guarantee a particular corporate actions role, employer, salary, city, or vacancy; review the current published terms before enrolling.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Read the trade lifecycle guide before applying for operations roles\",\"href\":\"/career-guides/trade-lifecycle/\",\"routeId\":\"career-guide-trade-lifecycle\"}]");
